 # Gradient Computation via Finite Differences
 
-Also called: Central Difference Gradient, Compute Gradient Map, Discrete Gradient, FD Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient Computation, Gradient Computation with Edge Clamping, Gradient Map Compute, Gradient Volume Generation, On-the-fly Gradient Computation, Tetrahedron Gradient Technique, Voxel Gradient Encoding, Voxel Gradient Estimation
+Also called: Central Difference Gradient, FD Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient, Finite Difference Gradient Computation
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient Computation via Finite Differences calculates the 3D gradient vector for each voxel in a scalar volume by approximating spatial derivatives using neighboring voxel values [S2, S4]. This method provides the surface normal information required for lighting models (such as [Gradient-based Diffuse Lighting with Alpha Blending](1257-gradient-based-diffuse-lighting-with-alpha-blending.md)) and isosurfacing algorithms when analytical gradients are unavailable [S4]. It is distinguished from analytical gradient computation by its reliance on discrete sampling and from tetrahedron-based methods by its direct use of axis-aligned neighbor differences [S1, S3].
+Gradient Computation via Finite Differences calculates the 3D gradient vector for each voxel in a scalar volume by approximating spatial derivatives using neighboring voxel values [S2, unofficial, S4]. This method provides the surface normal information required for lighting models (such as [Gradient-based Diffuse Lighting with Alpha Blending](1257-gradient-based-diffuse-lighting-with-alpha-blending.md)) and isosurfacing algorithms when analytical gradients are unavailable [S4]. It is distinguished from analytical gradient computation by its reliance on discrete sampling and from tetrahedron-based methods by its direct use of axis-aligned neighbor differences [S1, unofficial, S3, unofficial].
 
