 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Gradient Map Generation
-A specific GPU compute shader implementation that calculates gradients for all voxels and stores them in a 3D image buffer [S1]. It uses the global invocation ID to map threads to voxels, computes the gradient vector, normalizes it, and stores the direction in RGB and the clamped magnitude (multiplied by `grad_magnitude_modifier`) in the Alpha channel of an `rgba8_snorm` texture [S1].
+A specific GPU compute shader implementation that calculates gradients for all voxels and stores them in a 3D image buffer [S1, unofficial]. It uses the global invocation ID to map threads to voxels, computes the gradient vector, normalizes it, and stores the direction in RGB and the clamped magnitude (multiplied by `grad_magnitude_modifier`) in the Alpha channel of an `rgba8_snorm` texture [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel requires a constant number of reads and arithmetic operations [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient array (3 floats per voxel) or texture [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On GPU, dispatching compute shaders with workgroups of size 8x8x8 allows efficient parallelization [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; works on any discrete scalar field; provides necessary normal data for lighting and marching cubes/dual contouring [S4].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to noise in the volume data; central differences amplify high-frequency noise; boundary conditions require careful handling to avoid artifacts [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel requires a constant number of reads and arithmetic operations [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient array (3 floats per voxel) or texture [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On GPU, dispatching compute shaders with workgroups of size 8x8x8 allows efficient parallelization [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; works on any discrete scalar field; provides necessary normal data for lighting and marching cubes/dual contouring [S3, unofficial, S4].
+*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to noise in the volume data; central differences amplify high-frequency noise; boundary conditions require careful handling to avoid artifacts [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from standard numerical analysis techniques for approximating derivatives [S4]. In computer graphics, it is widely used in volume rendering for illumination [S3] and in isosurfacing algorithms like Dual Contouring, where CGAL's `Finite_difference_gradient_3` class provides a robust implementation for computing gradients from discrete values [S4]. The tetrahedron-based variant is attributed to Iñaki Ñiguez [S1].
